@h1 Migration from Markdown h1@

If you think in Markdown, you already think in Wit — you just haven't met the
vocabulary yet. Wit is a superset in spirit: everything Markdown expresses, plus
the structure Markdown makes you drop into raw HTML for. This page maps every
common Markdown construct to its Wit equivalent, then shows what Wit adds once
you've made the switch.

@h2 The one difference to learn first h2@

Markdown's inline emphasis is @strong symbolic strong@: a pair of asterisks or
underscores that must be matched, and escaped when you mean them literally. Wit's
emphasis is a @strong single mark that wraps one token strong@. Bold is one
asterisk, italic is one underscore:

@@@pre
This word is *bold* and this one is _italic_.
pre@@@

Because a mark wraps a token, it never fires by accident. Arithmetic and
identifiers stay plain with @strong no escaping strong@:

@@@pre
The product 3*4*5 and the file_name_here both stay literal.
pre@@@

That single change — one mark, token-scoped — removes most of the escaping you do
in Markdown out of habit. Keep it in mind and the rest of the mapping is
mechanical.

@h2 The mapping h2@

@h3 Headings h3@

Markdown's @@@code # Heading code@@@ through @@@code ###### code@@@ become
@@@code @h1 Heading h1@ code@@@ through @@@code @h6 Heading h6@ code@@@, rendering
@@@code <h1> code@@@ … @@@code <h6> code@@@.

@h3 Bold and italic h3@

@@@code **bold** code@@@ becomes @@@code *bold* code@@@ (one asterisk); both
@@@code *italic* code@@@ and @@@code _italic_ code@@@ become @@@code _italic_ code@@@
(one underscore). For both at once, put bold outside: @@@code *_both_* code@@@.

@h3 Lists h3@

A bulleted @@@code - item code@@@ becomes @@@code @ul @li item li@ ul@ code@@@; a
numbered @@@code 1. item code@@@ becomes @@@code @ol @li item li@ ol@ code@@@. The
@@@code @li code@@@ items carry the content; the @@@code @ul code@@@ or
@@@code @ol code@@@ decides bullets or numbers.

@h3 Links and images h3@

A link @@@code [text](url) code@@@ becomes @@@code @a |href url| text a@ code@@@ —
the address is metadata in a parameter, the text is the body. An image
@@@code ![alt](src) code@@@ becomes @@@code @img |src src| |alt alt| code@@@.

@h3 Inline code and code blocks h3@

Inline @@@code `code` code@@@ becomes @@@code @@code code code@@ code@@@, and a
fenced block becomes @@@code @@pre … pre@@ code@@@. The doubled @code @@ code@ is
Wit's @strong verbatim strong@ fence: inside it, everything is literal, so
@@@code @@code render(@doc) code@@ code@@@ keeps @@@code @doc code@@@ as text
instead of treating it as a reference — the equivalent of Markdown's backticks,
and just as safe.

@h3 Blockquotes and rules h3@

A quote @@@code > text code@@@ becomes @@@code @blockquote text blockquote@ code@@@,
and a horizontal rule @@@code --- code@@@ becomes @@@code @hr code@@@.

@h3 Tables h3@

A Markdown pipe table becomes a @@@code @table code@@@ node with its rows in a
parameter:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Site, Status], [Dunmore Head, operational]]| |caption Stations|
pre@@@

Note this is a @strong node call strong@, not a grid of pipe-and-dash rows. Pasting
a Markdown pipe table won't work — but you gain a real @@@code <caption> code@@@,
and, as you'll see below, the option to generate the whole table from data.

@h2 What Wit adds h2@

The mapping above reaches parity. Here's what you get that Markdown simply can't
express without leaving Markdown behind.

@h3 Captions on figures h3@

A figure with a real, semantic caption — no raw HTML:

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp|
  @figcaption The lamp that never went dark. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

@h3 Tables built from data h3@

Define your rows once as records, and render a table from them — change the data,
and the table changes:

@@@pre
#sites: [{ name - Dunmore Head, status - operational }, { name - Carrick Point, status - maintenance }] !!

@table |schema [name, status]| |rows @sites|
pre@@@

@h3 Reusable components h3@

Define a structure once and reuse it, with captured parameters and a body slot —
Markdown has no equivalent:

@@@pre
#chapter ||number, title||
@h2 ::number:: — ::title:: h2@
...
chapter#

@chapter |number I| |title The Keeper|
For eleven days the lamp had burned untended.
chapter@
pre@@@

@h3 Conditionals for draft-versus-final h3@

Show content only when the data warrants it — one source, many editions:

@@@pre
#book: { status - draft }

(if @book.status is draft)
@aside(class note) Draft — not for circulation. aside@
(end)
pre@@@

@h3 Self-organising documents h3@

Citations name an idea, not a page number. Define a source once and reference it
in prose:

@@@pre
#weil: Simone Weil, Gravity and Grace (1952) !!

Attention, as @weil argued, is the rarest form of generosity.
pre@@@

And a bibliography can gather its own entries with an additive definition — each
@@@code +#bibliography code@@@ appends to the list:

@@@pre
#bibliography: Weil, Gravity and Grace (1952). !!
+#bibliography: Berger, Ways of Seeing (1972). !!

@bibliography
pre@@@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Typing @@@code **bold** code@@@ out of habit. strong@ Wit bold is a single asterisk, so @@@code **bold** code@@@ gives you a literal @code * code@ on each side of a bold word. Use @@@code *bold* code@@@. li@
@li @strong Expecting @@@code _*x*_ code@@@ to nest. strong@ It leaves the inner @code * code@ literal. For bold-and-italic, put bold on the outside: @@@code *_x_* code@@@. li@
@li @strong Expecting a bare URL to auto-link. strong@ @code https://example.org code@ in prose stays plain text — Markdown auto-links it, Wit doesn't. Wrap it in @@@code @a |href …| … a@ code@@@ when you want a link. li@
@li @strong Pasting a Markdown pipe table. strong@ Wit tables are @@@code @table code@@@ node calls with rows in a parameter, not rows of pipes and dashes. li@
@li @strong Putting a comma inside a table cell. strong@ Cells in @@@code |rows [[…]]| code@@@ are comma-separated and can't be escaped — a comma splits the cell. Rephrase, or move the data into records. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

New to the node mechanic? Read
